{"id":"neet-2026-q018","pn":18,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","st":"Elastic moduli","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Young's Modulus — I. (Δd/ΔL)(L/d)\nB. Compressibility — II. FL/(A(ΔL))\nC. Bulk Modulus — III. −(1/ΔP)(ΔV/V)\nD. Poisson's Ratio — IV. −P(V/ΔV)\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"c":3,"s":"A. Young's modulus = stress/strain = FL/(AΔL), which is II.\nB. Compressibility = 1/(bulk modulus) = −(1/ΔP)(ΔV/V), which is III.\nC. Bulk modulus = −P(V/ΔV), which is IV.\nD. Poisson's ratio = lateral strain/longitudinal strain = (Δd/ΔL)(L/d), which is I.\nSo A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q008","pn":8,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","st":"Elastic limit and Young's modulus","q":"The maximum elongation of steel wire of 1 m length if the elastic limit of steel and its Young's modulus, respectively, are 8 × 10⁸ N m⁻² and 2 × 10¹¹ N m⁻², is-","o":["40 mm","8 mm","4 mm","0.4 mm"],"c":2,"s":"Elongation is maximum when the stress equals the elastic limit.\nδmax = (σ × L)/Y = (8 × 10⁸ × 1)/(2 × 10¹¹)\nδmax = 4 × 10⁻³ m = 4 mm."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q042","pn":42,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","st":"Thermal stress","q":"A metallic bar of Young’s modulus, 0.5 × 10¹¹ N m⁻² and coefficient of linear thermal expansion 10⁻⁵ °C⁻¹, length 1 m and area of cross-section 10⁻³ m² is heated from 0°C to 100°C without expansion or bending. The compressive force developed in it is:","o":["100 × 10³ N","2 × 10³ N","5 × 10³ N","50 × 10³ N"],"c":3,"s":"Longitudinal strain = thermal strain = αΔT.\nStrain δ = 10⁻⁵ × 10² = 10⁻³.\nCompressive stress = δ × Y = 10⁻³ × 0.5 × 10¹¹ = 0.5 × 10⁸ N m⁻².\nCompressive force = stress × area = 0.5 × 10⁸ × 10⁻³ = 0.5 × 10⁵ N.\nThis is 5 × 10⁴ × (10/10) = 50 × 10³ N."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q016","pn":16,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","st":"Stress","q":"Let a wire be suspended from the ceiling (rigid support) and stretched by a weight W attached at its free end. The longitudinal stress at any point of cross-sectional area A of the wire is:","o":["Zero","2W/A","W/A","W/2A"],"c":2,"s":"Longitudinal stress = internal restoring force/area.\nThe restoring force equals the external force W.\nSo stress = W/A.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":200,"alt":"Wire hanging from a rigid ceiling with a block W at its end: W acts downward and tension T upward on the wire.","d":[["l",50,20,150,20],["l",56,20,50,12],["l",66,20,60,12],["l",76,20,70,12],["l",86,20,80,12],["l",96,20,90,12],["l",106,20,100,12],["l",116,20,110,12],["l",126,20,120,12],["l",136,20,130,12],["l",146,20,140,12],["l",100,20,100,100],["l",100,100,100,60,"a"],["t",108,76,"T"],["r",80,100,40,36],["t",100,123,"W","m"],["l",100,136,100,178,"a"],["t",100,192,"W","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2022-q040","pn":40,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","st":"Shear modulus and elasticity of materials","q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R). Assertion (A): The stretching of a spring is determined by the shear modulus of the material of the spring. Reason (R): A coil spring of copper has more tensile strength than a steel spring of same dimensions. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","o":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)"],"c":1,"s":"When a coil spring is stretched, its length and volume do not really change; only its shape changes.\nSo stretching is decided by the shear modulus. (A) is true.\nSteel has more tensile strength than copper. So (R) is false."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q126","pn":126,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","st":"Young's modulus","q":"A wire of length L, area of cross section A is hanging from a fixed support. The length of the wire changes to L₁ when mass M is suspended from its free end. The expression for Young's modulus is :","o":["Mg(L₁–L)/(AL)","MgL/(AL₁)","MgL/(A(L₁–L))","MgL₁/(AL)"],"c":2,"s":"Young's modulus Y = stress/strain = (F/A)/(ΔL/L).\nF = Mg and the extension ΔL = L₁ − L.\nY = MgL/(A(L₁ − L))."}
